# SwiftUI views

- All new screens are SwiftUI with Observation. Do not add new `UIViewController` subclasses.
- The existing Top Movies list stays UIKit. Bridge it with `UIViewControllerRepresentable` when it has to live inside SwiftUI navigation.
- Observe with `@State private var viewModel` (injected at construction) or `@Environment`. Do not use `ObservableObject`, `@Published`, `@StateObject`, or `@ObservedObject`.
- A view is a function of state: `switch viewModel.state` and handle every case, including empty and failed.

```swift
switch viewModel.state {
case .idle, .loading: ProgressView()
case .loaded(let movies) where movies.isEmpty: EmptyStateView()
case .loaded(let movies): MovieList(movies: movies)
case .failed(let error): ErrorStateView(error: error) { await viewModel.load() }
}
```

- No networking, persistence, or business rules in `body`. Put derived display values on the view model.
- Extract subviews once `body` outgrows roughly one screen. Do not grow `@ViewBuilder` chains driven by booleans.
